# Research and sources

Follow `docs/source-policy.md`.

- Prefer official and contemporary evidence appropriate to the claim.
- Map every important claim to the most exact source location practical,
  distinguish publication date, event date, and public-knowledge time, and
  document why a precise locator is unavailable.
- Do not invent exact calendar dates; leave `publication_date: unknown` when
  needed. Use content-based availability to decide whether clearly pre-cutoff
  contemporary material may still support readable, attributed prose.
- Attribute expectations in published prose through natural narration
  (“the paddock expected…”); label expectation vs fact in research records,
  not with reader-facing meta such as “only an expectation / not the result.”
  Preserve unresolved disagreement.
- Never guess facts, silently combine incompatible accounts, or use a citation
  for more than it supports.
- Isolate cutoff-safe facts from later sources and flag spoiler-bearing sources.
- Quote briefly only when exact wording adds historical value.

Use the `historical-f1-research` and `source-verification` skills for substantial
work. The globs anticipate the provisional architecture; apply this rule
wherever historical research or claims are handled.
